body:
@danstam thanks for your patience. I've been busy with unitaryHACK issues.

As I've thought about this more, I lean more and more towards not fixing what's not broken. Right now we use deprecated numpy code, but it's working perfectly fine. Until the code is removed, I think we should not make any changes here. I'm just not seeing a huge advantage. If you have arguments for making the change I'm happy to hear it, but for now I'm going to close this PR (and the associated issue) and wait for the breaking change to appear in numpy removing the polynomial functions.

Sorry to close your contribution, but know you're welcome to contribute to other issues!

I'm going to go with #3041 for the code, but @nez0b made an important observation about the case when representations contain both positive and negative signs. For that reason I'd like to credit both of you for the solution.

@cosenal both hackers made important contributions to what is the final product being merged here. Can we give both hackers the bounty? Happy to provide the extra cash if that's the issue.

@zkasuran please comment here so you can be assigned.

@natestemen workaround for the mutable list is a normal tuple (no out-param, no list default). `construct_circuits(..., return_info=True)` now returns `tuple[DDDInfo, ...]` rather than a list.

Logging is on `construct_circuits` only: INFO on `mitiq.ddd`, one line per trial (`DDD: found N idle windows; inserted M sequences`). `insert_ddd_sequences` stays quiet so there is no double noise.

Ready for another look.
